    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Palmyra grew from a desert caravan station into a leading trading city in the first and second centuries CE, wealthy enough to build the Temple of Bel, the Great Colonnade and the tower tombs.</li><li>In the 260s King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odaenathus">Odaenathus</a> defeated the Sasanian emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a>, and his widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a> ruled as regent for her son and rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> until Aurelian defeated her and razed the city in 273.</li><li>The city shrank to a fortress, then a small village; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> destroyed it in 1400, and in 1932 the villagers were moved to a new village so that the ancient site could be excavated.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>At its height, around 270 in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a>, Palmyra had more than 200,000 residents, and by the beginning of the 20th century the village on the site had 6,000.</p></aside>
    </section>

