    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 2000 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Puzur-Ishtar the Tadmorean agrees to a contract at an Assyrian trading colony at Kultepe, the city's first appearance in the record</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">32</span><span class="rf-text">The Temple of Bel is dedicated</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">129</span><span class="rf-text">Hadrian visits, names the city Hadriane Palmyra and makes it a free city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">260</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odaenathus">Odaenathus</a> defeats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and becomes the ruler of a de facto allied kingdom</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">273</span><span class="rf-text">Aurelian razes the city after a rebellion</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">634</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> takes the city under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1400</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> attacks and destroys the city, reducing it to a small village</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1932</span><span class="rf-text">The villagers are moved to the new village of Tadmur and the ancient site is opened to excavation</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Palmyra lies in an oasis in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, 215 km north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, with the Syrian Desert to the south and east and two mountain ranges to the north and south-west. A small wadi, al-Qubur, crosses the area, and south of it is the spring of Efqa. Palmyra began as a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> settlement near the Efqa spring, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a> are dated to 7500 BCE. At Tell ez-Zor, near the spring, traces of settlement go back to the Prepottery Neolithic A period. A sounding in the tell beneath the Temple of Bel uncovered a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> structure built around 2500 BCE.</p><p>The city entered the historical record around 2000 BCE, when Puzur-Ishtar the Tadmorean agreed to a contract at an Assyrian trading colony at Kultepe. Tablets from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> of the 18th century BCE record the name as Ta-ad-mi-ir and mention the place as a stop for trade caravans and nomadic tribes, and Assyrian inscriptions of the 11th century BCE give it as Ta-ad-mar. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> records a desert city called Tadmor, built or fortified by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> attributed the founding of Palmyra to him. That association is a conflation of Tadmor with a city Solomon built in Judea, and Palmyra was a small settlement in the 10th century BCE. The origin of the name is unclear; the standard interpretation connects it to the Semitic word for date palm, tamar.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zenobia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zenobia.jpg" alt="Zenobia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antoninianus_of_Zenobia_(obverse).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>, between 312 and 64 BCE, Palmyra became a prosperous settlement. In the middle of the Hellenistic era it began to expand beyond the northern bank of the wadi, and by the late 2nd century BCE the tower tombs of the Valley of Tombs and the first city temples were being built. During the first century CE it developed from a minor desert caravan station into a leading trading centre, with Palmyrene merchants establishing colonies in surrounding trade centres. The city's first walled fortifications were built in that century, and the Temple of Bel was dedicated in 32 CE.</p><p>Palmyrene trade reached its peak in the second century, helped by a trade route that the Palmyrenes built and protected with garrisons, and by the Roman conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a> in 106, which moved control of the southern trade routes from the Nabataeans to Palmyra. Hadrian visited in 129, named the city Hadriane Palmyra and made it a free city. The projects of this period included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, the colonnade and the Temple of Nabu. At its height, around 270 in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a>, Palmyra had more than 200,000 residents. Its economy rested on agriculture, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pastoralism">pastoralism</a>, taxation and above all the caravan trade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">Silk</a>, exported from East to West, was the main income-earning good, and the Harbaqa Dam, built in the late first century CE, can collect 140,000 cubic metres of water.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pompey"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pompey.jpg" alt="Pompey" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Venice)_Pompey_the_Great,_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Didier Descouens, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Palmyra changed hands between several empires. It owed allegiance to the Seleucid king until 64 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> conquered the Seleucid kingdom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> established the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Palmyra was left independent, trading with Rome and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> but belonging to neither. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> sent a force against it in 41 BCE, and it became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> around 14 CE, early in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>, keeping much of its internal autonomy. In the 190s it was assigned to the new province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenice">Phoenice</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a> made it a colonia between 213 and 216.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odaenathus">Odaenathus</a> appears as lord of Palmyra in an inscription of 252. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a> defeated and captured the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a> in 260, Odaenathus won a victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and received from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallienus">Gallienus</a> the title Imperator Totius Orientis. Palmyra became in practice an allied kingdom. He was assassinated in 267 and succeeded by his ten-year-old son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaballathus">Vaballathus</a>, with the boy's mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a> as de facto ruler. From 270 she conquered Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, then invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>. Aurelian defeated her at Immae and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Emesa</a> and besieged Palmyra; Zenobia was captured and the city capitulated. After 273 Rome ruled it directly.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> took the city in 634, and it later belonged to the Umayyad and Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphates</a>. In the 12th century the Burids, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zengid-dynasty">Zengids</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> held it, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-fadl">Al Fadl</a> clan governed it. It came under Ottoman rule in the 16th century and, with Syria, under the French Mandate after 1920.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nero"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nero.jpg" alt="Nero" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nero_Glyptothek_Munich_321.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Palmyra's people combined elements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amorites">Amorites</a>, Arameans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>. The earliest known inhabitants were Amorites, Arameans were recorded by the end of the second millennium BCE, and Arabs arrived in the late first millennium BCE and were assimilated. The city also had a Jewish community, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> were few and restricted in their settlement. The Palmyrenes spoke Palmyrene <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> and used Greek for commercial and diplomatic purposes. The last Palmyrene inscription dates to 279/280, and after the Arab conquest Greek was replaced by Arabic.</p><p>Thirty clans are documented, and by the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> the city had four tribes, each living in a quarter bearing its name. A council of about six hundred members of the local elite supervised public works, approved expenditure, collected taxes and appointed two archons each year. Women commissioned inscriptions, buildings and tombs, and in some cases held administrative offices. The chief deity was Bol, later called Bel, with over sixty ancestral gods of the clans beside him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> replaced this worship, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> was reported in the city by 325.</p><p>The Palmyrenes buried their dead in family mausoleums, with a relief of the person interred on the wall as a headstone, and many tombs held mummies embalmed in a way similar to that of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The Great Colonnade, 1.1 km long, was the main street, and the large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agora">agora</a> resembled an eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserai</a> more than a Greek public square.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/diocletian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diocletian.jpg" alt="Diocletian" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Istanbul_Archaeology_Museum_Bust_of_Diocletian,_from_Nikomedia,_Izmit,_end_3rd_century_AD_1536.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dosseman, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The greatest disaster of the ancient city came in 273. Aurelian had spared Palmyra and left a garrison of 600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, but the city rebelled under Septimius Apsaios and declared Antiochus, a relative of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a>, Augustus. Aurelian marched against it, razed it, and took its most valuable monuments to decorate his Temple of Sol. Buildings were smashed, residents massacred and the Temple of Bel pillaged. Palmyra was significantly reduced and largely disappeared from the historical record. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> later restored it at a reduced size, with a wall enclosing about 80 hectares, much smaller than the city before 273.</p><p>Palmyra's loyalty to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> brought an aggressive military response from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>, and the city diminished in size, losing its merchant class. In 745 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marwan-ii">Marwan II</a> ordered its walls demolished. Earthquakes devastated it in 1068 and 1089. In 1400 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> attacked Palmyra; according to Sharaf ad-Din Ali Yazdi, his men took 200,000 sheep, and the city was reduced to a settlement on the desert border whose inhabitants herded animals and cultivated small plots.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrian-civil-war">Syrian civil war</a> the Islamic State captured Palmyra in May 2015 and destroyed large parts of the ancient city, among them the Temple of Bel and the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalshamin">Baalshamin</a>. It held the city again from December 2016 to March 2017.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Palmyra's first scholarly description appeared in 1695, and in 1751 an expedition led by Robert Wood and James Dawkins studied its architecture. Louis-François Cassas surveyed its monuments in 1785. Its funerary busts reached Western museums in large numbers in the 19th century and became evidence in an art-historical debate about Eastern influence on Roman art. The Palmyra Tariff, a stone slab of 137 CE inscribed in Greek and Palmyrene with the city's tax law, was discovered in the Tariff Court in 1882 and has been described as one of the most important single items of evidence for the economic life of any part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p><p>Henri Seyrig began large-scale excavation in 1929, starting with the Temple of Bel, and persuaded the villagers, who lived within the ruins, to move to a new French-built village next to the site. The move was completed in 1932, and the villagers settled in Tadmur. In 1980 the historic site, including the necropolis outside the walls, was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>. Most of Palmyra remains unexplored, especially the residential quarters in the north and south.</p><p>Palmyra today is an ancient site in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> beside the town of Tadmur. The Valley of Tombs west of the walls, a necropolis one kilometre long, holds more than 50 monuments, mostly tower tombs.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Palmyra is an ancient city in a Syrian oasis that grew wealthy from caravan trade. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odaenathus">Odaenathus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a> it ruled much of the Roman East until Aurelian razed it in 273. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> destroyed it again in 1400, and it is now a ruin beside Tadmur.</em></p>

